eceiver (param 0) is seeded as the record type with its :num fields surfaced\n  (the unit rich field types, via *field-type-box*), so dbl-arith? contagions a :num field\n  read that sits beside a proven :double operand \x2014; wrapping it in coerce :double,\n  emitted as jolt->fl. That is the same machinery a genuine ^double field\n  reaches; contagion is sound because Clojure double contagion makes the result a\n  double regardless, so eagerly converting the :num operand is value-identical\n  (the same rule dbl-arith? already encodes). A pure-:num expression (no proven :double operand)\n  contagions nothing \x2014; dbl-arith? requires a :double sibling.\n\n  eligible? is true only when contagion fired a site the shared (lean) path leaves\n  generic \x2014; more coerce :double nodes than the lean receiver type yields \x2014; so a clone\n  is emitted exactly when it recovers fl* the shared body lacks. Isolated from the\n  shared fixpoint: it reads the rich field types, never the lean, so pm-rets\n  and the ordinary impl body stay Option-A lean. Returns [arity false] when the\n  receiver isn't a known record (a host type, or no record shape)." _kc$36465 1411 _kc$36466 1 _kc$36467 "jolt/passes/types.clj")))) _kc$36468))))
